ng in the  classrooms and  the courses  i'm  sorry no no it's i mean  yeah i think you know  we um i think many universities were  already beginning to go online before  the pandemic particularly in in the  asynchronous mode  i did training to do that at my  university there was a lot of incentive  financial incentive for professors to  move teaching online  um and you know one thing i  noticed in the training is that you're  you're very you know they're telling you  essentially  to leave out anything remotely  resembling kind of scholarly expertise  like it's like sort of you have to  really like kind of  you know give people the the  three-minute recorded video  no longer than this and so on and it  hasn't been my experience that that's  necessarily what students want or are  capable of  you know of receiving um so i do think  that there's  we talk a fair bit and just the  conclusion to the book about  um you know some of the ways in which in  the modern research university  there is a whole industry built up now  around telling professors how to teach  right and giving them advice about  teaching um  yeah centers for teaching and learning  all over  but also um visits somebody another  interesting question on the on the chat  is  what about evaluation of teaching by  colleagues  in the course of a tenure review or a  promotion review  um how does that work  yeah i mean are we capable of  i'm just imagining an extension of this  question are we capable of evaluating  someone's teaching without understanding  this larger history  and seeing one person's classroom in the  context  of all these decisions that teachers  have made over time  right i like that idea that this can  help  that having a deeper sense of the  history could help a more  professional and contextualized  disciplinarily contextualized  evaluation of teaching it's interesting  because so  when people do look at classrooms  especially the earlier classrooms that  we've looked at in scholarly research  they tend to only look at  teaching evaluations so there is this  whole kind of interesting history  of teaching evaluations being used to  stand in for classrooms the way i think  they very much  are in 10 year decisions in which anyone  cares at all about them  it's funny there's this rumor going on  in in europe that  americans are given grades by their  students  and they're really appalled by it  sort of true  yeah i've got one i think we have time  for one more  question um did you  sense that there's some connection  between  teaching strategies and research  methods but also literary production  i mean do some people's way of teaching  actually feed right in it sounds like  it's true of elliot but  other examples of the way that teaching  inspires  literary production now i take it that  means not just critical production but  the production of literary works maybe  fiction or  yeah absolutely i think probably the  best example of this in the book is jay  saunders reading  who is teaching um you know  he's teaching at hampton in the 30s 40s  and 50s  courses like negron american literature  but also courses on the novel  he's also writing um in a couple of  different  genres that are you know realist um so  he's writing a documentary he's writing  a campus  novel um and he's sort of writing all  around  this kind of question of  realist representation you know as he's  teaching in the classroom so he's  teaching dickens thinking about how  dickinson's autobiography gets written  into some of his fiction  um at the same time that he's kind of  writing all around that  matrix as well so that was a good  example of where we really needed to  look at  the fiction and the literary production  in order to understand what he was doing  in the classroom  it's fascinating that you mentioned the  campus novel because it is a genre  that's just exploded over the last 30  years  so there is something fascinating about  the scene of teaching that appeals to  novelists  and all those campus novels are not  realist i mean some of them are just  wild it's a realist  i guess the obvious i mean the obvious  thing there is uh lecture courses that  get  basically kind of collated to become  um books often kind of introductory  books in  various fields but the question about  online teaching  uh definitely reminded me that you know  last semester i recorded my lectures  as audio lectures and never has teaching  felt more to me like writing than it did  then because because it was going to be  recorded  i found i can't do these you know i  can't sort of stand in front of the room  and vamp and look at a  picture together and interact with the  students  and because you know they could play it  back and so on i felt almost the level  of like writing for publication like  each word has to be  precise in its way and i i think there's  probably a ton of  examples of places where we could find  some  student question or student response to  a lecture or even the shape of a lecture  course that ended up changing that  person's book that was based on the  um on the lecture course that they gave  me  somebody once told me that when you  teach you just never know  what of all the stuff you say is going  to stay in the minds of your students  like some don't you remember some just  weird anecdote that some  teacher told about their dog and you've  forgotten the whole rest of the chorus  but you remember  that one anecdote absolutely  all right i'm ending on a  little light note here but i just  i want to thank you all so so much  for this conversation it was really  really fun and  and inspiring and uh thank you to  to all the participants for their  questions we'll go look on the chat  too to see your comments now that that  we're  we're done with the q a so get this book  y'all and read it  and please um we are gonna put the link  with the university of chicago press  discount code we're putting it in the  chat  thank you so much alice and thank you  thank you thanks and caleb
 

